<%# pb-24 rather than pb-12, and the number is the footer's. #277 pinned the rating scale to
    the bottom of the screen, and a fixed element is out of the flow -- so without padding
    the same height beneath it, it would sit on top of the last set row of every session.
    The one control it would cover is Done, which is the whole point of the screen.

    24 is 96px against a collapsed footer of 44px plus its safe-area inset, which is more
    than the arithmetic needs. That slack is deliberate: it is the gap between the last Done
    button and the footer, and a thumb aiming at the first needs to not find the second. %>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-md pb-24">
  <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-gray-900">
      <%= @workout[:date].strftime('%A %b %-d') %>
    </h1>
    <%# "record" was the noun -- the page that records this session -- and read as the verb,
        which on a gym screen is an instruction to start recording. It is also where you go
        when you have stopped lifting, so it says that instead (#216).

        It only navigated, though, and #218 is what that cost: nothing in the app could say
        a session was over, so a day finished with three of ten sets done looked exactly
        like one still under way -- to the workouts list, and to an assistant reading the
        history through MCP, which called it in progress. The words were already right. Now
        the control does what they say, and lands on the record either way, which is where
        the link went before.

        A button rather than a link because it writes, and a form rather than htmx because
        this is the one control on the screen whose whole job is to leave it. Styled as the
        link it replaces: it sits beside an h1 as a quiet way out, not as the thing the
        page is for -- that is Done, five times over, below. %>
    <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/session/finish" method="post">
      <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/session/finish") %>
      <button type="submit" class="min-h-11 text-sm text-gray-500 underline hover:text-lime-500">
        <%= @workout[:finished_at] ? 'finished' : 'finish workout' %>
      </button>
    </form>
  </div>

  <div id="session-body">
    <%== render('workouts/_session_body') %>
  </div>
</div>

<%# The rating scale, once, pinned to the foot of the screen. #277.

    Here rather than inside #session-body, and that is the placement the whole thing rests
    on: a Done tap sends back one lift panel and the progress bar, and the poll sends back
    every panel, so anything inside that region is replaced several times a session. A
    <details> is stateful -- it is open or it is not -- and state inside a swapped element
    is state that resets. Out here it is rendered once per page load and htmx never touches
    it, so a scale the lifter opened stays open through a set, a tap, and a poll.

    Asked of the whole session rather than of one lift, which is the guard that used to live
    at the foot of _lift_panel.erb. Same predicate -- ratable? on the model, so the table and
    the five buttons that fill it in can never disagree about who is being asked -- but read
    across every set on the page, because a footer belongs to the page.

    A session with nothing ratable in it therefore has no footer at all: all warmups, or all
    timed work, and the table would be explaining a scale nothing on screen offers. That
    session also keeps the shorter bottom padding it needs, which is the one thing the wrapper
    above cannot know -- and is why pb-24 is unconditional there rather than clever here. A
    little dead space at the foot of a warmup-only session is worth less than a rule that
    reads two ways. %>
<%- if @sets.any?(&:ratable?) %>
  <%== render('workouts/_rpe_help') %>
<%- end %>

<%# One thing left in here, and it is not the one that used to be.
    A tap used to replace the whole of #session-body, which destroyed the scroller along
    with everything in it and took its scrollLeft with it -- so finishing a set on lift
    three dropped the lifter back on lift one, every set, all session. This script existed
    to save that number across the swap and put it back, and its note explained at length
    why hx-preserve was the wrong answer.

    #235 made the swap a single lift panel instead. The scroller is no longer replaced, so
    its scroll position is never lost, and the whole save-and-restore went with the problem
    rather than being carried alongside it.

    What remains is marking which lift you are on in the progress bar, which is #212 and is
    a different job: the bar is re-rendered out of band on every tap, so the marks come back
    unset and have to be recomputed. Bound to document.body because htmx events bubble, so
    it binds once and survives every swap; and to document in the capture phase for scroll,
    because scroll events do not bubble at all.

    Which panel is centred is a scroll position, and CSS cannot see one, so this is set from
    JS. It degrades to the bar exactly as it was: without the script every segment is
    unmarked, which is what it looked like before, and the per-panel dots and "3 of 5" are
    rendered server-side and go on saying where you are regardless. %>
<script>
  (function () {
    function markCurrentLift() {
      var panels = document.getElementById('lift-panels');
      var sections = panels ? panels.querySelectorAll('section') : [];
      var segments = document.querySelectorAll('[data-lift-segment]');
      if (!panels || !sections.length || !segments.length) { return; }
      var width = sections[0].getBoundingClientRect().width;
      if (!width) { return; }
      var current = Math.round(panels.scrollLeft / width);
      for (var i = 0; i < segments.length; i++) {
        segments[i].setAttribute('data-current', i === current ? 'true' : 'false');
      }
    }

    document.addEventListener('scroll', function (event) {
      if (event.target && event.target.id === 'lift-panels') { markCurrentLift(); }
    }, true);

    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:afterSwap', markCurrentLift);

    markCurrentLift();
  })();
</script>

<%# The session clock, ticking. #281.
    The number in the header is true when the server sends it and stale a second later, so
    this counts on from it. It does not fetch anything and does not know what time it is on
    the server: the element carries how many seconds had already elapsed, and this adds the
    seconds since the page was painted. A phone whose clock is wrong is therefore still
    right, which asking the browser for a wall-clock start time would not be.

    Re-baselined on every htmx swap rather than left running, because the header comes back
    out of band on every tap and on every poll -- with a fresh figure from the server, which
    is the authoritative one. Reading the element again after each swap is what keeps the
    script agreeing with the page rather than drifting away from it over a long session.

    Bound to document.body because htmx events bubble, so it binds once and survives every
    swap. The same reasoning as the script above it, and the reason both live out here.

    Degrades to exactly what the server sent: with JavaScript off the header shows the
    session length as at page load, which is a true statement about a real session rather
    than a blank. That is why the figure is rendered server-side at all.

    The format is deliberately the same shape as Timing.phrase, and deliberately not shared
    with it -- one is Ruby on the server and one is JavaScript in the browser, and the only
    way to have one copy would be to ship a number and a formatter over the wire. The two
    can drift; spec/timing_spec.rb pins the Ruby half, and the server re-renders on every
    tap, so a drift shows up on screen rather than hiding. %>
<script>
  (function () {
    var clock = null, base = 0, painted = 0;

    function spell(total) {
      if (total < 120) { return total + 's'; }
      var minutes = Math.floor(total / 60);
      if (minutes < 60) { return minutes + 'm'; }
      return Math.floor(minutes / 60) + 'h ' + (minutes % 60) + 'm';
    }

    function rebase() {
      clock = document.getElementById('session-clock');
      if (!clock) { return; }
      base = parseInt(clock.getAttribute('data-elapsed'), 10) || 0;
      painted = Date.now();
    }

    function tick() {
      if (!clock || !clock.isConnected) { return; }
      clock.textContent = spell(base + Math.floor((Date.now() - painted) / 1000));
    }

    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:afterSwap', rebase);
    rebase();
    setInterval(tick, 1000);
  })();
</script>
